// Seed-keyed malicious ciphertext binding of a KEM, single-key (SAMEKEY)
// case: the adversary chooses the SEED the key pair is derived from, and
// must present two distinct ciphertexts that decapsulate to the same
// shared secret under the derived decapsulation key.
//
//   Breakable:   Challenge(seed, ct0, ct1) derives
//                [ek, dk] = DeriveKeyPair(seed) and returns
//                Decaps(dk, ct0) == Decaps(dk, ct1) && ct0 != ct1
//   Unbreakable: Challenge always returns false
//
// Relation to CDM24 (Cremers, Dax, Medinger, ePrint 2023/1933). Their
// X-BIND-K-CT family has X in {HON, LEAK, MAL}: honest keys with a
// decapsulation oracle, honest keys handed out, or adversary-chosen
// keys. This notion sits between LEAK and MAL: the adversary controls
// the key-generation coins but the key is always a well-formed output
// of DeriveKeyPair. It is implied by CDM24's MAL-BIND-K-CT (Figure 6): an
// adversary here yields one there that picks mode g = 1, derives the
// pair from its seed, and submits it twice with the same ciphertexts. It
// is strictly weaker than that game (neither malformed keys, nor the
// two-key case, nor the Encaps modes are assumed) and, for uniformly
// random seeds, it is LEAK-BIND-K-CT in the single-key case. Two
// differences of presentation from the paper: Decaps in this directory's
// KEM primitive takes no separate pk, so the paper's (sk, pk) collapses
// to dk; and explicit rejection is not modelled, so the paper's check
// that neither key is bot is vacuous, as it is anyway for the implicitly
// rejecting KEMs the draft targets.
//
// Why this notion, and why keyed by the seed. LEAK-BIND-K-CT of a seeded
// hybrid (CG_seedbased, CK_seedbased) hands the adversary the hybrid
// seed, and with it every component decapsulation key. A reduction to
// the LEAK notion of KEM_PQ would have to place the challenger's PQ key
// pair inside a seed whose PRG expansion yields it, which is possible
// only with the PRG modelled as a programmable random oracle
// (proofs/{CG,CK}/*_seedbased_LEAK_BIND_K_CT_SAMEKEY.proof). Under a
// malicious-key notion the reduction generates the hybrid seed itself
// and submits what it derived, so nothing has to be embedded and the
// proof is in the standard model
// (proofs/{CG,CK}/*_seedbased_LEAK_BIND_K_CT_SAMEKEY_std.proof). The
// draft's expandDecapsKey always computes dk_PQ as DeriveKeyPair(seed_PQ),
// so the reduction only ever holds a seed-derived PQ key; keying the
// assumption by that seed states exactly what is used and rules out
// malformed keys, which is where the known malicious-key attacks live.
//
// What is known for ML-KEM. CDM24 Table 5 lists ML-KEM as LEAK-BIND-K-CT
// only, because Schmieg (ePrint 2024/523, Algorithm 2) breaks
// MAL-BIND-K-CT with TWO decapsulation keys: the second is a well-formed
// key whose cached public-key hash h is replaced by the first key's, so
// that G(m || h) yields the same (K, r) under both and Encrypt(pk_i, m; r)
// gives two different accepted ciphertexts. That attack is inherently
// two-key and does not reach this single-key notion. For one key, even a
// malformed one, Schmieg's Proposition 1 argument applies verbatim: both
// decapsulations reject gives K = J(z || ct_i), a J collision; one rejects
// gives a cross-hash collision between J and G; both accept gives a G
// collision, hence the same (m, r), and deterministic re-encryption then
// forces ct0 == ct1. The single-key cases of Fiedler and Guenther's
// Theorem 3.10 (ePrint 2024/702), which proves ML-KEM LEAK+r-BIND-SS-
// {CT,PK} with the key-generation randomness revealed, are the same
// argument and use no randomness of the key. So ML-KEM meets this notion
// with adversarially chosen seeds, under collision resistance of G and J
// (a random-oracle-style bound for the cross case).
//
// That argument is mechanized in this directory:
// proofs/MLKEM/MLKEM_MALSEED_BIND_K_CT_SAMEKEY.proof proves
// MALSEED_BIND_K_CT_SAMEKEY(MLKEM) for schemes/MLKEM/MLKEM.scheme --
// FIPS 203's Fujisaki-Okamoto transform over an abstract deterministic
// K-PKE (primitives/DetPKE.primitive), with SHA3-256, SHA3-512 and
// SHAKE256 as standard-model Hash primitives -- with the bound
//     Adv^CR(J) + Adv^cross(G, J) + Adv^CR(G's key half),
// one term per case of the argument above, and no assumption on the
// lattice layer. So a hybrid proof that assumes this notion of KEM_PQ can
// discharge it for ML-KEM by that lemma.
//
// The same is NOT true of a seed-keyed K-PK notion: FIPS 203 takes the
// rejection value z straight from the 64-byte seed (d || z), so two seeds
// sharing z give Schmieg's Algorithm 3 attack even for seed-derived keys.
// The hybrid K-PK proofs need no PQ assumption, so this does not affect
// them.

import '../../../primitives/KEM.primitive';

Game Breakable(KEM K) {
    Bool Challenge(BitString<K.Nseed> seed, K.Ciphertext ct0, K.Ciphertext ct1) {
        [K.EncapsKey, K.DecapsKey] [ek, dk] = K.DeriveKeyPair(seed);
        K.SharedSecret ss0 = K.Decaps(dk, ct0);
        K.SharedSecret ss1 = K.Decaps(dk, ct1);
        return ss0 == ss1 && ct0 != ct1;
    }
}

Game Unbreakable(KEM K) {
    Bool Challenge(BitString<K.Nseed> seed, K.Ciphertext ct0, K.Ciphertext ct1) {
        return false;
    }
}

export as MALSEED_BIND_K_CT_SAMEKEY;
